 Chapter 6 Anatomy of a Prompt A prompt is the main lever a practitioner controls when using a pre-trained model without retraining it. This part of the book treats prompting as an engineering discipline rather than a collection of tricks: Anatomy (this chapter): what a prompt is made of and how to lay it out. Core techniques: clarity, examples, structure, structured output, reasoning, and prompt chaining, together with the evidence for each. Strategy cards by use case: extraction, classification, summarization, coding and SQL, grounded question answering, agents, marketing copy, and LLM-as-judge prompts. Guideline-first prompt building: collect authoritative standards first, then draft, test, and revise a prompt against them. Evaluation and maintenance: success criteria, test sets, graders, and regression testing. Robustness and safety: prompt injection, data leakage, and defences. The guidance is vendor-neutral. Where a vendor’s documentation is cited, it is cited as evidence of current practice, not as a universal rule. Model behaviour and product features change quickly, so check the linked documentation before relying on a product-specific detail. 6.1 What a prompt is For a given request, the prompt is everything the model conditions on before it generates: system or developer instructions, the conversation so far, supplied documents, tool results, and the user’s latest message. The model then produces a completion \(y\) by sampling from a conditional distribution \(p(y \mid x)\), where \(x\) is the prompt. Three consequences follow directly from that definition: A prompt changes the distribution, not the model. The weights are fixed. A better prompt shifts probability towards useful completions; it does not add knowledge the model lacks or guarantee a correct answer. Outputs vary. Under sampling (Section 1.6), the same prompt can produce different answers. One good response is an anecdote, not evidence that the prompt works. Everything in the context competes for attention. Irrelevant material is not neutral; it can distract the model and costs tokens. The context engineering chapter (Chapter 22) develops this point for agents. Prompting is therefore closer to designing an experiment than to writing a command: specify the task carefully, then measure whether the outputs meet a predefined standard. 6.2 Messages and roles Chat-style models receive a list of messages, each with a role. The names vary by vendor, but the structure is similar: Role Written by Typical content System / developer The application builder Purpose, standing rules, output format, examples User The end user (or the application on their behalf) The request and the variable input for this call Assistant The model Previous model responses in the conversation Tool result A tool the application ran Search results, query output, file contents Vendor naming in current documentation: Anthropic passes standing instructions in a separate system parameter and the conversation in messages (prompting best practices). OpenAI calls the application builder’s role developer and describes a chain of command in which developer messages take priority over user messages (prompt engineering guide). Google Gemini calls them system instructions (prompt design strategies). OpenAI’s guide offers a useful analogy: developer and user messages relate like a function and its arguments. Stable rules belong in the system or developer message; the material that changes from call to call belongs in the user message. One rule matters more than any naming convention: content is not instructions. A customer review, a retrieved web page, or a tool result is data to be processed. Text inside it that looks like a command (“ignore your previous instructions”) should not be obeyed. Delimiting data clearly is the first defence; the robustness chapter later in this part covers the rest. 6.3 The building blocks Vendor guides list slightly different components, but they converge on six building blocks. Not every prompt needs all six; a quick question may need only the first. Block Question it answers Typical failure when missing Task What should the model do? The model answers an easier or different question Context Why is this needed, and for whom? Generic output that ignores the real goal Input data What should it work on? The model invents or assumes the material Examples What does a good answer look like? Inconsistent format, tone, or labelling Output format What shape should the answer take? Output that is hard to parse or compare Constraints What is out of bounds? Unwanted libraries, labels, length, or claims 6.3.1 Task State the action and its object in the first lines: “Assign one theme to each survey response” rather than “Here are some survey responses.” Use a numbered list when the order or completeness of steps matters. Anthropic’s guide offers a practical test: show the prompt to a colleague with no background on the task and ask whether they could follow it. If they would be confused, the model probably will be too. 6.3.2 Context and motivation Context includes the audience, the purpose, and the reason behind each rule. Explaining why lets the model generalize to cases the rule did not anticipate. Anthropic’s example contrasts “NEVER use ellipses” with an explanation that the output will be read aloud by a text-to-speech engine that cannot pronounce them; the second version tells the model what to do in related situations as well. 6.3.3 Input data Supply the material the model must work on rather than expecting it to recall facts. A model asked about “our Q3 campaign” without the campaign data can only guess. Wrap inputs in clear delimiters so the model can tell data from instructions. 6.3.4 Examples Examples are among the most reliable ways to fix format, tone, and labelling conventions. They should be relevant to the real inputs, diverse enough to cover edge cases, and clearly separated from the instructions. Examples also carry unintended signals: if every example is short, outputs become short; if every example is positive, the model may under-predict negatives. The core-techniques chapter of this part treats few-shot design in detail. 6.3.5 Output format Specify the shape you can check: a JSON object with named keys, a table with fixed columns, or a fixed set of labels. A format you can parse is a format you can evaluate automatically. 6.3.6 Constraints Constraints bound the answer: allowed labels, language and library versions, length, reading level, and claims the model must not make. State what to do instead, not only what to avoid (“Use ‘other’ when no theme applies” rather than only “Don’t invent themes”). 6.3.7 What about roles and personas? A one-sentence role in the system prompt (“You are a market-research analyst”) can focus tone and vocabulary, and current vendor guides still recommend it. Treat it as a modest steering device. A persona does not add expertise the model lacks, and claims that a particular persona reliably improves accuracy should be tested on your own task rather than assumed. 6.3.8 Mapping to the earlier question framework The What, Why, How, Constraints structure in the prompting chapter of the Claude part (Chapter 13) maps onto these blocks: What is the task, Why is the context, How is the output format and examples, and Constraints are constraints. The six-block version adds input data and examples as separate, deliberate design choices. 6.4 An annotated example Suppose the marketing team wants open-ended survey responses grouped into themes for a monthly brand-health report. A first draft often looks like this: Classify the survey response by theme. The agent on the phone solved it in five minutes. The model must guess which themes exist, how many to assign, what format to return, and what to do when nothing fits. Different calls will guess differently, so monthly counts will not be comparable. A structured version separates stable instructions (system message) from the variable input (user message): SYSTEM &lt;task&gt; Assign one theme to each open-ended customer survey response. &lt;/task&gt; &lt;context&gt; The themes feed a monthly brand-health report for the marketing team. Consistent labels matter more than nuance, because counts are compared month to month. &lt;/context&gt; &lt;constraints&gt; - Use exactly one of: price, delivery, product_quality, service, other. - Use &#39;other&#39; when no theme clearly applies; do not invent new themes. &lt;/constraints&gt; &lt;output_format&gt; JSON: {&quot;theme&quot;: &quot;&lt;label&gt;&quot;, &quot;evidence&quot;: &quot;&lt;short quote&gt;&quot;} &lt;/output_format&gt; &lt;examples&gt; &lt;example&gt; &lt;input&gt;Arrived two weeks late.&lt;/input&gt; &lt;output&gt;{&quot;theme&quot;: &quot;delivery&quot;, &quot;evidence&quot;: &quot;two weeks late&quot;}&lt;/output&gt; &lt;/example&gt; &lt;example&gt; &lt;input&gt;Great taste, but too pricey.&lt;/input&gt; &lt;output&gt;{&quot;theme&quot;: &quot;price&quot;, &quot;evidence&quot;: &quot;too pricey&quot;}&lt;/output&gt; &lt;/example&gt; &lt;/examples&gt; USER &lt;input&gt; The agent on the phone solved it in five minutes. &lt;/input&gt; What each part contributes: The task names one theme per response, removing the multi-label ambiguity. The context explains why consistency matters, which guides borderline decisions. The constraints fix the label set and give the model a legitimate exit (other), which reduces invented categories. The output format asks for an evidence quote, so a reviewer can audit each label quickly. The examples demonstrate the format. Two is the minimum for illustration; a production prompt would include more, covering other and ambiguous cases. The structured prompt is not guaranteed to be better. It is testable: its outputs can be parsed, counted, and compared with human labels. Whether it outperforms the draft is an empirical question for the evaluation chapter. 6.5 Order and layout Separate stable from variable content. Put rules, format, and examples in the system or developer message, and the per-call input in the user message. This makes prompts easier to version and review. Several providers also cache repeated prompt prefixes, so a stable opening section can reduce cost and latency. Long material first, question last. For long documents, Anthropic and Google both recommend placing the documents near the top and the specific question or instruction at the end. Anthropic reports that putting queries at the end improved response quality by up to 30 percent in its tests with complex, multi-document inputs (long context prompting). Treat that figure as the vendor’s internal result, not a guarantee for your task. Use delimiters consistently. XML-style tags (&lt;context&gt;, &lt;input&gt;) and Markdown headings both work; consistency matters more than the choice. Tags are especially useful for marking where untrusted data starts and ends, and for nesting multiple documents with their sources. Write in a normal register. Older prompts often shouted: “CRITICAL: You MUST…”. Anthropic’s current guidance notes that newer models are more responsive to system prompts and that such emphatic language can cause over-triggering; a plain instruction usually suffices. 6.6 Calibrating the system prompt The building blocks say what a system prompt contains. A separate decision is how specific each instruction should be. Anthropic’s context engineering guide calls this finding the “right altitude” between two failure modes: hard-coding brittle if-then logic, and giving high-level guidance that wrongly assumes the model shares your context (effective context engineering). The trade-off applies to any long-lived system prompt, whether for an assistant or an agent. Take an assistant that answers account managers’ questions about weekly paid-media performance. Its system prompt could be written at three levels: Too specific About right Too vague Looks like Dozens of if-then rules: “If CTR is below 0.8%, write ‘CTR is below benchmark’. If CPA rises more than 15% week on week, use template B. Always return exactly five bullets.” A role, the goal, a way to structure answers, a few principles with their reasons, and when to hand off “You are a helpful marketing analytics assistant. Answer questions about campaign performance.” Works when The task is narrow and an exact behaviour is required, such as a legal disclaimer Most assistants and agents that face varied questions Quick, one-off questions where the model’s defaults are acceptable Fails when A case no rule covers arrives (a new channel, a budget-pacing question); rules start to contradict each other Principles are missing for a frequent case; testing shows where The model must guess the date window, the attribution model, or whether to speculate about causes; answers vary from run to run Fix Replace each cluster of rules with the principle behind it; keep hard rules only where exactness matters Add a principle or an example for each recurring failure Add what a new colleague would need: the data, the definitions, the audience, the limits The “about right” version, written out: SYSTEM You help account managers understand weekly paid-media performance for their clients. They repeat your answers in client calls, so accuracy and clear caveats matter more than speed. Data: the weekly_performance table (spend, impressions, clicks, conversions, revenue by campaign and channel). Use the metric definitions in the glossary below, not platform defaults. How to answer: 1. Answer the question in one or two sentences. 2. Show the numbers behind it, with the date window and comparison period. 3. Suggest drivers only if the data supports them, and say how confident you are. Principles: - Compare like with like (same weekday mix, same attribution window), because mismatched periods produce false alarms. - Small campaigns are noisy; treat changes based on a few dozen conversions as tentative. - Say &quot;the data does not show this&quot; rather than guessing. Hand off to the account lead when: - the question needs data outside this table (CRM, offline sales); - the answer would recommend a budget change or a client commitment. &lt;glossary&gt; ... &lt;/glossary&gt; Each principle carries its reason, so the model can apply it to cases nobody wrote a rule for, such as a holiday week or a channel added last month. Hard rules remain where exactness matters: the hand-off conditions are stated plainly, not as heuristics. Altitude is not length. The “about right” prompt is longer than the vague one and may be longer than the brittle one; what matters is that each line is a definition, a principle, or an example rather than a patch for one past failure. The too-specific prompt is usually the kitchen-sink mistake (Section 6.9) seen from the other side. As with every prompt choice, test the result, and include inputs that no rule anticipated. 6.7 Offline example: assembling a prompt from parts Treating a prompt as data rather than a hand-edited string makes it easier to check, version, and reuse. The class below stores the building blocks, warns about missing ones, and renders the system and user messages. It makes no API call. from dataclasses import dataclass, field @dataclass class PromptSpec: task: str context: str = &quot;&quot; output_format: str = &quot;&quot; constraints: list[str] = field(default_factory=list) examples: list[tuple[str, str]] = field(default_factory=list) def check(self): &quot;&quot;&quot;Return warnings for missing or weak building blocks.&quot;&quot;&quot; warnings = [] if not self.context: warnings.append(&quot;No context: the model must guess purpose and audience.&quot;) if not self.output_format: warnings.append(&quot;No output format: responses will vary in shape.&quot;) if not self.examples: warnings.append(&quot;No examples: consider 3-5 for format-sensitive tasks.&quot;) return warnings def system_prompt(self): &quot;&quot;&quot;Stable instructions, r