umulated over months. Long prompts are not wrong, but each rule should earn its place through a failure it prevents. One-run validation. Declaring success after a single good output. 6.10 Checklist Before using a prompt for anything that matters: The task is stated in the first lines, as an action on an object. The context explains the purpose and audience, and the reasons for non-obvious rules. Input data is supplied and clearly delimited from instructions. Examples, if used, are relevant, varied, and consistent with the rules. The output format is specific enough to parse or check. Constraints say what to do instead, not only what to avoid. Stable instructions and variable inputs are separated. Success criteria and a few test inputs exist before iteration begins. 6.11 Exercise: dissect a prompt Take a prompt you used recently at work or in study. Label each sentence with one of the six building blocks. Which blocks are missing? Rewrite it with stable instructions in a system section and the variable input in a delimited user section. Write five test inputs, including one ambiguous case and one case where the correct answer is “none of the above”. Run both versions on the test inputs several times and compare consistency. Keep the results; the evaluation chapter of this part reuses them. 6.12 Key takeaways A prompt conditions the model’s output distribution; it does not change the model or guarantee correctness. Roles separate the builder’s standing instructions from the user’s request and from untrusted data. Six building blocks (task, context, input data, examples, output format, constraints) cover most prompts; use the ones the task needs. Put stable material first and the per-call input or question last; delimit data consistently. Write system prompts at the right altitude: principles with reasons, not a pile of if-then rules or a one-line brief. A structured prompt is valuable mainly because it is testable. 6.13 References Anthropic. Prompt engineering overvie