 Chapter 8 Reflection People rarely send the first draft of anything important. They reread it, notice that “next week” is ambiguous or that a figure is wrong, and fix it. Reflection gives an LLM workflow the same step. The model, or a second model, reviews an output and produces an improved version. Reflection is the easiest agentic pattern to add to an existing system. It is also the easiest one to overrate. This chapter explains how it works, when it helps, why it often fails without outside information, and how to measure whether it is worth its cost. 8.1 The basic pattern A reflection workflow has two or more LLM calls in sequence: Generate. Prompt the model for a first draft (version 1). Critique. Pass version 1 to a model with a different prompt. Ask it to review the draft against stated criteria and list the problems. Revise. Ask the model to write version 2 that fixes those problems. Steps 2 and 3 are often combined into a single “review and rewrite” prompt. Keeping them separate makes the critique visible, which helps with debugging and evaluation. The critique step does not always lead to a rewrite. It can also filter: generate twenty candidate taglines, then have the critic keep only the three that meet every criterion. Brainstorm-then-filter is often cheaper and safer than asking for one perfect answer. Example. An analyst asks a model to draft a short client email summarizing a campaign’s results. The first draft says “performance improved last month” and quotes a click-through rate that does not match the report. A reflection prompt that says “check every number against the attached report and replace vague time references with dates” catches both problems. The revised draft names the month and uses the correct figure. The same structure works for many outputs: Output What the critique step looks for Email or report Tone, accuracy of facts and promises, missing details, length Code Bugs, unhandled edge cases, readability Structured data (deeply nested JSON, HTML tables) Malformed structure, missing fields, wrong types Step-by-step instructions Missing or out-of-order steps, ambiguity Names, taglines, or brand copy Hard to pronounce, unintended meanings in the target markets’ languages SQL query Wrong joins, wrong filters, missing date ranges 8.1.1 Same model or a different one? The critic can be the same model with a different prompt, or a different model. Different models have different strengths. A common configuration drafts with a fast, cheap model and critiques with a reasoning model, because models that think before answering tend to be better at spotting bugs and logical gaps. Treat the choice of generator and critic as a configuration to test, not a fixed rule. Background. Critique-and-revise also appears at training time. Constitutional AI (Bai, Kadavath, et al. 2022) has a model critique and revise its own responses against written principles, then trains on the revisions (Section 4.6). Reflection as discussed here happens at inference time and changes no weights. In short. Reflection is generate, critique, revise. The critique can also filter a set of candidates instead of rewriting one. The critic can be the same model or a different one; test which works. It happens at inference time; Constitutional AI uses the same idea at training time. 8.2 What the evidence says Reflection is not magic. Its effect ranges from large to zero to negative, depending on the task and on whether the critic has new information. Where self-feedback helps. Self-Refine (Madaan et al. 2023) used one model to generate, give feedback on, and refine its own output, with no extra training and no external tools. Across seven tasks (including dialogue responses, code optimization, code readability, and constrained generation), humans and automatic metrics preferred the refined outputs. Task performance rose by about 20 percentage points on average. Where it does not. In the same paper, math reasoning barely moved: GPT-4 went from 92.9% to 93.1% on GSM8K, and GPT-3.5 did not improve at all. The authors traced this to the critic. A reasoning chain that looks consistent convinces the model that everything is fine. ChatGPT’s feedback was essentially “everything looks good” for 94% of the math problems. When an external signal told the model that its answer was wrong, the gains exceeded 5 points. Huang et al. (2024) tested intrinsic self-correction, where the model reviews its own answer with no external feedback, on reasoning benchmarks. Performance did not improve and sometimes fell. For GPT-3.5 on GSM8K, accuracy went from 75.9% to 75.1% after one round of self-correction and to 74.7% after two, because the model changed some correct answers to wrong ones. They also showed that some earlier reports of self-correction gains had used ground-truth labels to decide when to stop, which a deployed system does not have. Where external feedback makes the difference. Reflexion (Shinn et al. 2023) has an agent reflect in words on task feedback, such as the results of unit tests, and store the reflections in memory for its next attempt. It reached 91% pass@1 on HumanEval, against 80% for GPT-4 used directly. CRITIC (Gou et al. 2024) has the model check its output with tools (search for facts, a code interpreter for programs) before revising. It improved question answering, mathematical program synthesis, and toxicity reduction, and its authors stress that the external feedback is what matters. Why the pattern looks like this. A critic that sees only the draft has the same knowledge and blind spots as the model that wrote it. It catches problems that are easier to recognize than to avoid, such as a missing sign-off, a wrong format, a forgotten requirement, or awkward phrasing. It tends to miss subtle reasoning errors, and it may “fix” correct answers to please the reviewer prompt. Models trained on human preferences are prone to this kind of agreeable over-correction (Sharma et al. 2024). New information breaks the symmetry: the critic now knows something the generator did not. The studies side by side: Study Feedback the critic gets Result Self-Refine (Madaan et al. 2023) None (self-feedback) About +20 points on average across seven tasks; almost nothing on math Huang et al. (Huang et al. 2024) None (intrinsic self-correction) No gain on reasoning; GPT-3.5 on GSM8K fell slightly Reflexion (Shinn et al. 2023) Unit-test results, kept in memory 91% pass@1 on HumanEval vs. 80% for GPT-4 alone CRITIC (Gou et al. 2024) Search results, code interpreter Gains on QA, math programs, and toxicity reduction The pattern: the more outside information the critic has, the more reliable the gain. In short. Self-critique helps on writing, code style, and format, where problems are easy to recognize. It fails on reasoning, and can turn right answers wrong, when the critic has nothing new to go on. External feedback is what makes the gains consistent. 8.3 Reflection with external feedback External feedback means information from outside the model that is added to the critique step. It is the single most effective way to make reflection work. Typical sources: Source What it reveals Example Code execution Errors, stack traces, wrong outputs Run the generated script; pass the traceback to the critic Unit tests Which behaviors fail Return failing test names and their assertion messages Validators Schema or format violations A JSON-schema validator reports missing fields Rule checks in code Policy violations A regular expression finds competitor names in a draft reply Counters Length limits Count the words and report “212 words, limit is 150” Search or retrieval Facts the draft got wrong Retrieve a source and pass the relevant passage to the critic Database execution Empty, huge, or failed results Run the SQL query and report the row count or error A human Anything a person notices A reviewer marks a paragraph as off-brand Two of these deserve a comment. Length. LLMs are poor at hitting exact word counts, because they see tokens, not words. A few lines of code that count the words and report the overshoot fix this more reliably than any amount of prompt tuning. Fact checking. Suppose a draft states that BLEU and ROUGE were both introduced in 2002. A retrieval step finds that BLEU dates from 2002 (Papineni et al. 2002) and ROUGE from 2004 (Lin 2004). Passing those passages to the critic turns a plausible error into a specific, fixable one. Example: the external-feedback loop in code. The loop below writes a SQL query, runs it, and feeds any error or suspicious result back to the model. It stops when the checks pass or after three rounds. The helper names are illustrative. def answer_with_sql(question, schema, max_rounds=3): query = llm(f&quot;Schema:
