 runs slow Cause: Section 2 has expensive operations Fix: # Before: Load full dataset (1M rows) df = pd.read_csv(&#39;data.csv&#39;) # After: Sample for development df = pd.read_csv(&#39;data.csv&#39;, nrows=10000) # Change back to full data when finalizing 20.4.3 Issue: Results don’t match when rerun Cause: Missing random seed setting Fix: # In Section 1 Setup import random np.random.seed(CONFIG[&#39;random_state&#39;]) random.seed(CONFIG[&#39;random_state&#39;]) # In model training model = RandomForestClassifier( random_state=CONFIG[&#39;random_state&#39;] ) 20.5 Asking Claude About Notebooks I&#39;m building a data science notebook for [task]. Current structure: SECTION 1: [what&#39;s in setup] SECTION 2: [what&#39;s in analysis] SECTION 3: [what&#39;s in outputs] Requirements: - [feature 1] - [feature 2] - [must be reproducible] Can you: 1. Review the structure 2. Suggest improvements 3. Show example cells Keep it professional and well-commented. 20.6 Exercise: Restructure an Existing Notebook Take one of your notebooks: Identify the sections (setup, analysis, outputs) Reorganize by section (even if messy) Add CONFIG dict with all paths and parameters Add section headers (markdown cells) Test: Restart kernel, run all, verify it works Compare before/after. Notice how much more organized it is! 20.7 Performance Tips 20.7.1 Tip 1: Use Sampling for Development # Quick iteration df_work = df.sample(n=10000, random_state=42) # Final run df_work = df.copy() 20.7.2 Tip 2: Cache Expensive Operations # First time: compute if not os.path.exists(&#39;processed_data.csv&#39;): df_processed = expensive_operation(df) df_processed.to_csv(&#39;processed_data.csv&#39;) else: # Subsequent times: load cached version df_processed = pd.read_csv(&#39;processed_data.csv&#39;) 20.7.3 Tip 3: Profile Slow Code import time start = time.time() # your code end = time.time() print(f&quot;Took {end - start:.2f} seconds&quot;) 20.8 Key Takeaways ✅ Use 3-section structure (Setup, Analysis, Output) ✅ Make Section 1 complete and standalone ✅ Run all from top every time ✅ Set random seeds and record environment versions for reproducibility ✅ Prevent leakage by splitting before learned preprocessing ✅ Compare held-out performance with a baseline ✅ Add progress messages and headers ✅ Save and document outputs ✅ Test with “Restart &amp; Run All”

 B Agentic AI Labs: Walkthroughs The course Agentic AI (DeepLearning.AI, Andrew Ng) pairs each design pattern with hands-on labs. This appendix records what each lab built and what it teaches, chapter by chapter. It is written in my own words. The course notebooks are not reproduced: the code blocks below are short sketches of the idea a lab teaches, not runnable copies of the lab. The graded assignments are described by the concept they practise only. No solutions or exercise answers appear here. About the results. Outcomes below are as described in the course labs, not reproduced here. The labs call hosted models, so each run can give a different answer, and the model names they use (GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, o4-mini, 2025) will date quickly. Read the outcomes as illustrations of a pattern, not as benchmarks. B.1 Common setup Item Choice Model access aisuite, a thin wrapper that calls models from several providers through one OpenAI-style chat interface Models OpenAI models of 2025: a fast model for drafting, a stronger reasoning model for critique Data and services Small local datasets (a coffee-sales CSV, a SQLite table of product events), a mock email server, and free public APIs for location and weather Code execution Generated Python is run with exec() inside the notebook. Fine for a lab, not for production (Section 9.4.1) Output Charts saved as PNG files; tables shown as data frames B.2 Reflection labs These labs go with Chapter 8. B.2.1 Lab: chart generation with multimodal reflection Goal. Turn a plain-English request into a chart, then improve the chart by letting a multimodal model look at the rendered image (Section 8.4). Steps: Generate code (V1). Ask a model for matplotlib code that answers a request such as “compare first-quarter sales in two years”. The prompt lists the data frame’s columns with their types and asks for the code inside &lt;execute_python&gt; tags, saving the chart to a fixed file name. Run it. Extract the tagged code and execute it with only the data frame in scope. The result is a PNG. Reflect on the image. Send the PNG, the V1 code, and the original request to a vision-capable model. It returns a one-line JSON critique, then revised code in the same tags. Run V2 and compare the two charts. Wrap it as one workflow with separate model choices for generation and reflection. The extraction and execution step is a few lines: import re CODE = re.compile(r&quot;&lt;execute_python&gt;(.*?)&lt;/execute_python&gt;&quot;, re.DOTALL) def run_tagged_code(llm_output: str, df) -&gt; None: match = CODE.search(llm_output) if match is None: raise ValueError(&quot;no tagged code in the model&#39;s reply&quot;) exec(match.group(1), {&quot;df&quot;: df}) # lab only: no isolation, no time limit The critic’s instructions, paraphrased: You are reviewing a chart made for this request: {request} The chart image is attached; the code that produced it is below. Line 1: JSON {&quot;feedback&quot;: &quot;&lt;what to fix and why&gt;&quot;}. Then: revised code inside &lt;execute_python&gt; tags that saves to {path_v2}. Lessons: The image is the external feedback. The critic sees what a reader would see (overlapping labels, a misleading chart type) rather than guessing from the code. Split the models by job. A cheap, fast model drafts; a stronger reasoning model critiques. The critique step is where judgement pays. The critic may change the chart type, for example from bars to lines. That is a judgement call, not a bug, which is why chart quality should be scored with a rubric across many requests (Section 8.7), not by looking at one example. Watch the prompt for patches. The lab’s prompts carry several emphatic “CRITICAL” rules about date types, each fixing a crash seen in earlier runs. Stating the schema clearly up front is the durable fix; a growing list of emphatic rules is the too-specific end of Section 6.6. Parse defensively. The lab’s code tries to read the JSON line, then falls back to searching for any JSON object in the reply. Models do not always follow output formats exactly. B.2.2 Lab: SQL generation with reflection Goal. Show that reviewing a query’s text is not enough. The critic also needs to see what the query returned (Section 8.3). Data. One SQLite table of product events, not product states. Each row is an insert, restock, sale, or price update. The quantity change is positive when stock comes in and negative for a sale. Total sales must be derived from the sale events. Steps and outcome, for the question “Which product colour has the highest total sales?”: Version What the critic sees Outcome described in the lab V1: generate only No critic Valid SQL that sums quantity × price over the events; the “total sales” comes out negative V2a: reflect on the text Question, schema, V1 SQL The query looks reasonable on paper; the total is still negative V2b: reflect on the result The same, plus V1’s output table The critic notices the negative total and fixes the sign; the answer becomes positive A typical V1 and a robust version: -- V1: runs, but sale rows carry negative quantities SELECT color, SUM(qty_delta * unit_price) AS total_sales FROM transactions GROUP BY color ORDER BY total_sales DESC LIMIT 1; -- Robust: keep only sale events, then flip the sign SELECT color, SUM(-qty_delta * unit_price) AS total_sales FROM transactions WHERE action = &#39;sale&#39; GROUP BY color ORDER BY total_sales DESC LIMIT 1; A fix that only wraps the quantity in ABS() would still be wrong: insert events also have positive quantities and a price, so they would be counted as sales. Filtering on the event type is what makes the query mean “sales”. The difference between V2a and V2b is a single input to the critic: def critique_sql(question, schema, sql, result=None): prompt = f&quot;Question: {question}
